course: everyday
lesson: 511
title: Family parties, aunts and uncles, and the row nobody explains
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In Newcastle, Nan turns 70 and there are 40 relatives, a DJ, a buffet with sausage rolls stacked like bricks, and Uncle Gaz doing the Macarena in a shirt two sizes too small. «Howay, Ellie-May, get up!» («howay» is Geordie for come on). Ellie-May wants the floor to swallow her, then gets up and dances anyway. Family parties are loud, embarrassing and great, often all at once. In Ohio, Lorcan goes to a family reunion, meets cousins he never knew he had, and plays baseball with them all day.
explain: Aunts and uncles can be special people. In Belfast, Uncle Paddy pulls a coin from Ronan's ear at every single party. Ronan is 10 now and knows exactly how the trick works (the coin is hidden in Paddy's hand all along), and he still laughs, because the laugh is for Paddy. In London, Kwesi's uncle comes from Ghana for a wedding wearing kente, a bright woven cloth, and dances all night; Kwesi's mates see the photos: «Your uncle's drip is crazy» (drip means stylish clothes). In Swansea, Auntie slips Sadie a fiver every visit. Mum: «Oh, you don't have to!» Auntie: «Hush, it's for the cariad» (Welsh for darling).
explain: Big families mean big attention. In Bradford, Hamid goes to a wedding with 300 guests and gets his cheeks pinched by about 20 aunties: «Mashallah, so big now!» («Mashallah» is said to admire something good, and means roughly «God has willed it».) He's fed up, but smiles: it is love, just very squeezy love. In Melbourne at the Christmas barbie, Islay's uncle says, «Yeah nah, the cricket's rubbish this year,» and her aunt says, «Nah yeah, it's alright.» In Aussie talk «yeah nah» means no and «nah yeah» means yes, so they don't agree at all.
explain: Some families have a row nobody explains. In Kent, Aunt Jo never comes to anything. Tasmin asks why, and Mum says, «Long story.» Later Tasmin overhears that they «fell out over Grandad's will» (a will is a paper saying who gets someone's things after they die). Grown-ups often don't tell kids the details, and fixing it isn't a child's job. In Cork, at a funeral, Siobhan's auntie and her mam don't speak. Siobhan's cousin whispers, «Our mams hate each other.» Siobhan: «We don't have to.» They become friends. Kids don't have to carry the grown-ups' rows.
explain: And sometimes a party gets uncomfortable. In Inverness at Hogmanay (Scottish New Year), Ruaridh's uncle drinks too much, gets loud and falls over the coffee table. Some people laugh; Ruaridh doesn't feel like laughing. He goes and finds his mam and stays near her. That's exactly right: stay near a grown-up you trust.
fact: Family parties can be loud, embarrassing and great at the same time.
fact: Aunts, uncles and older relatives can be special people in a child's life.
fact: Relatives who come from another country can bring clothes, music and customs to be proud of; kente is a bright woven cloth from Ghana.
fact: Big family events can mean lots of hugs, cheek-pinching and attention; it is usually love, and you can still say politely if you don't like being touched.
fact: Relatives often give small gifts or money to show love; saying thank you is the right answer.
fact: In Australian talk «yeah nah» means no and «nah yeah» means yes.
fact: Some families have rows that last years; children are usually not told the details, and fixing them is not a child's job.
fact: A will is a paper that says who gets a person's things after they die; arguments over wills sometimes split families.
fact: Children don't have to carry the grown-ups' rows; cousins can be friends even if their parents aren't.
fact: Some grown-ups drink too much at parties; if it makes you uncomfortable, stay near a grown-up you trust.
fact: «Howay» is Geordie for come on; «drip» means stylish clothes; «cariad» is Welsh for darling; «Mashallah» is said to admire something good; Hogmanay is Scottish New Year; a «barbie» is an Aussie barbecue.
exercise: How many relatives were at Nan's 70th? | 40 | 70 | 14 | 400
exercise: What did Uncle Gaz shout to Ellie-May? | «Howay, Ellie-May, get up!» | «Sit down, Ellie-May, you're blocking» | «Turn that music down, I'm trying to eat» | «Fetch me a sausage roll, pet»
exercise: Ronan knows how Uncle Paddy's coin trick works. Why does he still laugh? | The laugh is for Uncle Paddy | He forgets the trick every year | He finds coins really funny | He's scared of upsetting his mam
exercise: What is kente? | A bright woven cloth from Ghana | A dance they do at Ghanaian weddings | A spicy rice dish from West Africa | A drum played at family parties
exercise: Kwesi's mates say, «Your uncle's drip is crazy». What do they mean? | His clothes look great | He spilled his drink | His dancing is weird | He's sweating loads and loads
exercise: Why does Auntie give Sadie a fiver? | To show love | Because Sadie asked for it | To pay her for chores | Because Mum told her to
exercise: What does «cariad» mean in Welsh? | Darling | Pocket money | Auntie | Cake
exercise: Why did Hamid smile while 20 aunties pinched his cheeks? | It was love, just squeezy | They paid him a pound each to | He enjoys having his cheeks pinched | His dad promised him a treat
exercise: What does «Mashallah» show when the aunties say it? | They admire something good | They're telling him off for growing | They want him to go away | They're saying sorry
exercise: In Australian talk, what does «nah yeah» mean? | Yes | No | Maybe | Go away
exercise: Why doesn't Aunt Jo come to family events? | A row over Grandad's will | She's busy on Saturdays with her work | She doesn't like Tasmin | She is always on holiday
exercise: What is a will? | A paper saying who gets a person's things after they die | A list of birthday presents for the family | A letter of complaint about a relative | A plan for the next family party
exercise: Whose job is it to fix Aunt Jo's row? | Not Tasmin's | Tasmin's, she's the youngest | Tasmin's, she asked about it | Grandad's, from his grave
exercise: What did Siobhan say when her cousin whispered «Our mams hate each other»? | «We don't have to» | «Then I hate you as well» | «I'll tell my mam you said that» | «Let's never speak again»
exercise: What did Lorcan do at the family reunion? | Played baseball with new cousins | Sat in the car all afternoon | Argued with his uncle about sport | Went home early because he was bored
exercise: What did Ruaridh do when his uncle got drunk at Hogmanay? | Went to find his mam and stayed near her | Laughed with everyone at the coffee table | Poured the rest of the drinks down the sink | Hid outside in the cold on his own
test: Islay's uncle: «Yeah nah, the cricket's rubbish». Aunt: «Nah yeah, it's alright». Do they agree? | No, he says it's rubbish, she says it's alright | Yes, they both say the cricket is rubbish | Yes, they both say the cricket is alright | It's impossible to tell from those words
test: Auntie visits Sadie every 2 weeks and gives £5 each time. The first visit is in week 2. How much does Sadie get by the end of week 10? | £25 | £50 | £20 | £10
test: «Howay man, ye'll dance or ye'll get nae cake!» Uncle Gaz is laughing. Is it a real threat? | No, it's a joke to get her up | Yes, she'll really get no cake | Yes, she'd better hide | No, he's angry with her
test: Nan's party: 40 relatives sit at tables of 6, and every table must be full except one. How many tables are needed? | 7 | 6 | 8 | 40
test: Tasmin: «If I ring Aunt Jo and tell her to say sorry, it'll be sorted, innit». What's most likely? | It's not her job, and it probably won't fix it | It will fix a years-long family row in a single phone call | Aunt Jo will come to the next party | Mum will thank her for interfering
test: Siobhan's cousin: «Me mam says your mam's a right wagon» (Irish for a horrible woman). What's Siobhan's wise answer? | «That's their row, not ours» | «Your mam's worse, so there» | «Right, we're not friends now» | «I'll go and tell my mam that»
test: An uncle at Hogmanay is staggering about with a drink. What would most likely happen if Ruaridh stayed right next to him? | He might get knocked over or upset | The uncle would sober up straight away | Nothing, drunk people are always fine | Everyone would think he was a hero
test: Hamid, fed up: «If one more auntie pinches my cheek I'm gonna scream». What's a better move? | Smile, or say politely he'd rather not | Pinch the aunties' cheeks back | Hide in the toilets all night | Scream really loud at the next one
test: In a cartoon, the family party ends with everyone friends forever and all rows fixed. What's real life like? | Some rows last years, and that's not on the kids | Every family row gets fixed at one party | Families never row in real life | Kids are always told the whole story
test: Kwesi's mate: «why's your uncle wearing a curtain lol». What's a good answer? | «It's kente, from Ghana, and it's well nice» | «I dunno, he's dead weird, just ignore him, mate» | «He lost his suitcase at the airport» | «Shut up or I'll sort you out»
test: Ronan's little cousin, 5: «Uncle Paddy found money IN Ronan's ear, he's magic!» Where was the coin really? | Hidden in Paddy's hand all along | Growing inside Ronan's ear | Made out of thin air by real magic | Fallen out of Ronan's hair
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the responsibility of a child in respect of a protracted dispute between adult relatives? | The child bears no obligation to resolve it | The child is obliged to mediate between the parties | The child must take the side of his or her parent | The child must sever contact with the relatives' children
exam: Identify the most appropriate conduct of a child at a family celebration at which an adult relative has become intoxicated. | To remain near a trusted adult | To remain beside the intoxicated relative to supervise him | To dispose of all the remaining beverages without consent | To depart alone into the street until the celebration ends
exam: Ascertain the meaning conveyed by an Australian speaker who responds «nah yeah» to a question. | Agreement | Disagreement expressed with great emphasis | Uncertainty as to the correct answer | A refusal to answer the question put
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a document termed a will. | It states who receives a person's possessions after death | It records the wishes of a child for birthday presents | It is a formal complaint lodged against a relative | It sets out the arrangements for a forthcoming party
